Our team lead wants a thread-safe config manager and I wrote this singleton in Java 17. It feels a bit heavy for what it does, though the concurrency part seems right to me. What's your overall impression?

```java
public final class ConfigManager {
    private static volatile ConfigManager instance;
    private final Map<String, String> values = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();

    private ConfigManager() {}

    public static ConfigManager getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (ConfigManager.class) {
                if (instance == null) {
                    instance = new ConfigManager();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }

    public String get(String key) { return values.get(key); }
    public void set(String key, String value) { values.put(key, value); }
}
```

